diff --git a/codex-rs/app-server/README.md b/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
index d64c2303f9..0afdec3b9c 100644
--- a/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
+++ b/codex-rs/app-server/README.md
@@ -113,3 +113,25 @@ Attachments record the resources currently associated with a thread, independent
 `thread/attachment/list` accepts one `threadId` and returns at most 100 attachments per page, ordered by creation time and attachment id. Continue with `nextCursor` and the same `threadId` until the cursor is `null`. Each thread can retain up to 100 attachments. Removing an attachment frees a slot for a new attachment.

 Attachment creation and deletion requests using the same thread ID are serialized across connections. The requesting client receives its response before the compact update is broadcast, and duplicate creates or absent deletes do not emit updates. Deleting the owning thread removes its attachments under the same lifecycle exclusion; queued attachment mutations then report that the thread was not found.
+
+## Host-managed realtime input
+
+Pass `clientManagedInputs: true` to receive normalized voice inputs through
+the existing item notification stream. The start response confirms
+`clientManagedInputs: true`; clients requiring this ownership must check that
+confirmation before delivering the SDP answer to the browser. Older servers may
+ignore an unknown request field.
+
+Managed inputs arrive through
+`thread/realtime/itemAdded` instead of starting or steering backing turns internally.
+The item has `type: "input.requested"`, a unique `inputId`, `realtimeSessionId`,
+`text`, and `source` (`{ type: "handoff", handoffId }` or
+`{ type: "transcriptTail" }`). The text remains untrusted conversation input;
+normalization does not grant authority to execute it. The client durably accepts
+it, prepares its normal turn context, then uses `turn/start` or `turn/steer`.
+No acknowledgement request, native wait queue, or implicit backing turn is created.
+An enabled transcript-tail flush uses the same input notification. Clients must
+fence notifications by their live session and decide whether to accept input
+while closing; notification delivery itself is not durable acceptance. Omitting
+the flag preserves native start-or-steer behavior. Input delivery and the output
+flag below are independent.
